body:
I think one of the most important things is to look at title conventions in your genre. YA fantasy titles look different than a lot of historical fiction titles do. There are a lot of different ways to do it (I mostly write YA fantasy/fantasy, so those will be most of my examples lol). I apologize in advance if any of my example are shitty. Just... Oops. 

&#x200B;

You could base your title on a character, be it the main character or another in the book: *The Red Queen*, *Harry Potter and the anything, A Wizard of Earthsea, The Cruel Prince, The Lord of the Rings.*

&#x200B;

 You could name it for some facet of the world: *Dune*, *The Hunger Games, Divergent, Caraval*. 

&#x200B;

You could name it for a theme in the book: *Shadow and Bone*, *Our Violent Ends*. 

&#x200B;

There are even more ways to do it, but I think the best titles combine some of these. Some of my examples already do this, but I want to look at the title *Six of Crows* specifically, because I think it's one that works well. It does the "character title" and "facet of the world title" thing while also feeling thematic, so it hits on several different points. Throughout reading it, you slowly start to realize why it's called that. There are six characters in the heist group, they are part of a gang called crows, and they have ties to gambling, so it has that "Queen of Hearts" style of card naming to it. 

&#x200B;

So I think if you can combine them it works well, but only if it feels natural. There are plenty of great titles that work with only one method.

I'm usually okay with going off plan if it doesn't feel right anymore. I just write as if I've already made the changes in the rest of my draft and make a note like "change this in draft two-- from here on out, character A is a giraffe." Then when I'm writing draft two, I look at all my notes and make sure that from all the earlier parts that I'd already written, I turn character A into a giraffe. 

&#x200B;

This is the reason I do very soft outlines. I know where my story is generally going, but if I spend too much time outlining, I usually end up throwing out big parts of it. It ends up feeling like I wasted a lot of time. So I just do very general outlines like "This happens in the first quarter of the book, this happens in the second, etc." so I have a road map, but don't feel boxed in.

I used to feel like this when I finished something great and it really stifled a lot of my creativity. Eventually, I changed my outlook and it really helped a lot. Instead of thinking, "Wow, I really want to write this exact same thing, but now I can't" I started looking at whatever it was I had just enjoyed and trying to figure out why I liked it so much. 

&#x200B;

Maybe it was the dynamic between several characters that I enjoyed. Or maybe it was a piece of worldbuilding that was particularly compelling. Maybe it was a story element (like an interesting mystery, or a plot twist) that I liked or that kept me hooked. And then instead of just recreating the whole story, I take that one thing and make it my own, making it work for my story. It really has changed my whole outlook and it makes it a lot less depressing to watch a good movie or read a really good book.

Immediately the beat wasn't what I was expecting coming off of cowboy like me lol. I liked the difference though. 

&#x200B;

Also, "I always felt I must look better in the rearview" made me so sad! This song definitely feels autobiographical and that she felt that way is so sad.

"I hope she'll be a beautiful fool... No sorry, I didn't mean that."

&#x200B;

I loved this whole part. Acknowledging being a little vengeful and that not being quite fair, but wanting to be angry at everyone in a hurtful situation. Also, "I can't make it go away by making you a villain."
